      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did cīma mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: పక్షి",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "పక్షి (pakṣi) — \"a bird\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "పక్షి (pakṣi) is the general one: any bird at all, the కోడి (kōḍi) included. It came in from Sanskrit, and beside it Telugu keeps its own పిట్ట for the small ones about a garden."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is a split this book has met before — an inherited word doing the everyday work and a borrowed one carrying the wider idea, the same shape as నిప్పు beside అగ్ని. Once you start expecting it, half the vocabulary sorts itself."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five animals now, from the గేదె (gēde) at the gate to the చీమ (cīma) under it, and a నమస్కారం for whoever keeps them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five: గేదె (gēde), మేక (mēka), కోడి (kōḍi), చీమ (cīma), పక్షి (pakṣi). Enough to greet a house and name what lives in it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
